repository-code: "https://github.com/binshuangli/uplift-bench"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - uplift modeling
  - heterogeneous treatment effects
  - causal machine learning
  - evaluation metrics
  - benchmark design
  - model selection
  - policy evaluation
  - Qini coefficient
abstract: >
  UpliftBench is an outer-test-isolated, multi-objective benchmark for uplift modeling
  (conditional-average-treatment-effect estimation). It evaluates 12 estimators
  across seven dataset families (synthetic, IHDP x10, Jobs x10, and four marketing
  RCTs), with ACIC 2016 as an independent boundary-case validation family, scoring
  each dataset by the metric that is actually knowable (root-PEHE or policy risk
  where treatment effects are known; the Qini proxy only on marketing RCTs).
  Findings: on the standard continuous benchmark (IHDP, the primary continuous
  panel), cumulative-gain Qini shows no detectable alignment with effect accuracy
  across all 100 realizations (mean rank correlation +0.00, 95% CI [-0.10, +0.10])
  while AUUC is more aligned (paired gap +0.56 [+0.45, +0.66]); the failure is not
  a tail effect and appears on one of three evaluated continuous families (absent
  on ACIC 2016 and on the heavier-tailed Revenue-Synthetic family, where the gap
  reverses); on Jobs, ranking metrics are structurally insufficient for
  sign-threshold policies, and in a within-sample split-rotation analysis direct
  policy-risk selection yields lower benchmark regret than random while the Qini,
  AUUC, and uplift-at-k selectors do not. Both findings are bounded: the first is
  not observed on the mild-tailed ACIC 2016 family, and the second vanishes under
  a budgeted-value objective where rank suffices.
